  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1218 AM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nOverall, the forecast next week is looking relatively dry with near \naverage temperatures. Models are starting to get into slightly \nbetter agreement that a quick-hitting trough will traverse New \nMexico on Monday. While ensemble guidance is trending further south, \nthe bulk of precipitation will still remain north of the CO/NM \nborder. The northern mountains should squeeze out a couple more \ninches of snow, but more than that looks unlikely given the speed at \nwhich this storm will be moving.\n\nRidging returns Tuesday of next week, but the progressive pattern \ncontinues as another quick-hitting trough starts to take shape late \nin the week. The ENS and GEPS continue to favor a faster-moving \npositively tilted trough that would bring nothing more than a dry \ncold front to New Mexico while the GEFS mean shows a neutrally \ntilted trough that could draw just enough moisture for some light \nrain/mountain snow. Agreement is high that ridging will amplify over \nthe desert southwest late week into the weekend, but a strengthening \nPacific jet will begin to break down the ridge early next week. It \nis unclear how soon that will translate to more unsettled weather \nover New Mexico, but long-term sub-seasonal guidance continues to \npoint towards wetter and colder weather during the early part of \nDecember.",
